doc.para:
Statements
　A statement ends with a ; (semicolon). Some statements consist of an expression followed directly by a semicolon, while others have a semicolon after a control structure such as if.

	a++;
	func1();
	return a+b;
	etc.

Blocks
　A block groups multiple statements written between { }. Statements are executed from top to bottom.

	{
		a++;
		b++;
	}

	{
		var t=func1();
		func2(t);
		break;
	}


　A ; (semicolon) is not required after a block.

　In addition, every block creates a variable scope.

Execution Order
　In TJS, statements written outside of functions, classes, and properties are executed as the script runs. Statements inside functions, classes, and properties are not executed until they are called.

	var a; // Executed immediately

	function test()
	{
		a+=10; // Not executed until called
	}

	a+=30; // Executed immediately


　More precisely, class definitions, function definitions, and property definitions are registered first when the script runs, and then the remaining script elements are executed from the beginning. Therefore, functions, classes, and properties can be forward-referenced within the same script block.

	test(); // Even if a function is declared after this position, it can be referenced if it is within the same script block

	function test()
	{
		inform("yah!");
	}


　Calling functions across different script blocks is possible as long as the target function has already been loaded at the time the function call is attempted.
　Also, variable declarations are processed in the order they are loaded, so forward referencing is not possible.
